WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.100
Here is a made -up neuron, resting at minus −71 millivolts.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.000
Potassium current leaves it all the time, about 200 picomperes.

3
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.700
Sodium current enters it all the time, also about 200 picomperes.

4
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.080
Neither ion is at equilibrium.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.640
Yet the voltage does not move.

6
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.380
Why?

7
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.830
Each ion has an equilibrium potential.

8
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.730
That is the voltage where the electrical force on the ion balances its concentration

9
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.370
gradient. Here the values are supplied.

10
00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:37.510
For potassium, EK is minus −95 millivolts.

11
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.290
For sodium, ENa is plus 60 .6.

12
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.150
Driving force is the distance from equilibrium.

13
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:49.250
For potassium it is plus 23 .8 millivolts, so the current goes outward.

14
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.350
For sodium it is minus 131 .8, so the current goes inward.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.660
Current is conductance times driving force.

16
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.500
Potassium has 8 .47 nanosemons of open leak channels.

17
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.460
That gives about +202 picoamperes outward.

18
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.000
Sodium has only 1 .53 nanosemons.

19
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.680
But its driving force is much larger.

20
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.760
It gives −202 picoamperes inward.

21
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.480
The two cancel. Rest means zero net current, not zero current for each ion.

22
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.590
Left alone, these leaks would run the gradients down.

23
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.090
The sodium -potassium pump puts the ions back, three sodium out and two potassium

24
00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.170
in. Its main job is keeping the gradients.

25
00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.350
So rest is a steady state held up by the pump.

26
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.170
It is not an equilibrium.

27
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:45.153
The Goldman -Hodgkin -Katz equation finds the voltage where the passive currents sum

28
00:01:45.153 --> 00:01:49.813
to zero. Each ion counts in proportion to its relative permeability.

29
00:01:50.493 --> 00:01:55.293
In this made -up neuron, potassium is 1 and sodium is 0 .04.

30
00:01:55.993 --> 00:01:59.093
The result is minus 71 .2 millivolts.

31
00:01:59.453 --> 00:02:02.093
That's its 24 millivolts positive of EK.

32
00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:04.573
The small sodium leak holds it there.

33
00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:09.383
Now double the sodium permeability, to 0 .08.

34
00:02:09.903 --> 00:02:13.523
The balance point moves to minus 58 .9 millivolts.

35
00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:16.623
That is 12 millivolts toward ENa.

36
00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:21.983
Raising one ion's permeability moves the voltage toward that ion's equilibrium

37
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:27.023
potential. Other cells have other ratios, so there is no universal resting

38
00:02:27.023 --> 00:02:28.783
potential. Keep this rule.

39
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:32.563
Rest is where the leaks cancel, and permeability sets where.
